# The worked rules of docs/rules-design.md, in the surface.
#
# These are §8's <R1> and <R2> as printed, and a trust rule that is not in the
# document but falls out of §13: turning `the user says it is raining` into
# `it is raining` is a rule, never a hard-wired intake, so it can be argued
# with and overridden.

# §8 <R1>. Written as `causes` when there were two connectives, because the
# effect had to land in a LATER moment and persist -- water you have stopped
# heating stays boiled. There are no moments, so there is nothing for that to
# mean here: what a corpus wants to say about persistence it says in its own
# rules, over its own vocabulary of time.
#
# The `-liquid($w)` is now an ERASURE, which is what it always wanted to be:
# the water stops being liquid, and afterwards nothing anywhere says it is.
rule <boil> = implies(
    { +heat($a, $w), +water($w), no boiling($w) },
    { +boiling($w), -liquid($w) } )

# §8 <R2>. Written as `implies` so that the backward reader could not produce a
# plan to make it rain by making it cloudy. That guarantee went with the second
# connective, and it is worth saying plainly rather than pretending otherwise.
#
# `@likely` was a GRADE on the conclusion. Grades are gone: what a rule says
# about the strength of its conclusion is now *in* the conclusion, and a rule
# can read it. Nothing believes `rain(monday, afternoon)` bare until something
# crosses the modality, which is a rule a corpus writes.
rule <weather> = implies(
    { +cloudy($day, morning), no likely(rain($day, afternoon)) },
    { +likely(rain($day, afternoon)) } )

# §13. The consequent is a bare variable: whatever the channel says, believe it
# -- but only as `likely`, which is the rule's contribution and is a claim
# rather than an annotation. A corpus can ask which of its beliefs came in this
# way, and could not when it was a grade.
#
# The sign went out of `says` with the entry: a channel reports what it heard.
rule <trust_user> = implies(
    { +says(user, $p), no likely($p) },
    { +likely($p) } )

fact +liquid(kettle)
fact +heat(anna, kettle)
fact +water(kettle)
fact +cloudy(monday, morning)

# An arrival on a channel. What is written is that the channel said so; the
# rule above is what makes it a claim about the world.
say user: +raining(here)
